In music, '''sharp''' – eqv. '''{{lang|fr|dièse}}''' (from [[French language|French]]) or '''diesis''' ({{langx|el|δίεσις}}){{efn|
For the etymology of the words {{lang|fr|dièse}}, ''diesis'', and {{math|δίεσις}}, ''see'' [[diesis]].
}} – means higher in [[pitch (music)|pitch]]. The sharp symbol, ♯, indicates that the [[note (music)|note]] to which the symbol is applied is played one [[chromatic semitone]] higher. The opposite of sharp is [[flat (music)|flat]], indicating a lowering of pitch.  The symbol [[Accidental (music)#History of notation|derives from a square form of the letter ''b'']].

{{Short description|Symbol indicating one semitone lower}}{{Redirect-distinguish-text|♭|the Latin lowercase letter [[b]], the Zhuang letter [[Ƅ]], nor the Cyrillic soft sign [[Ь]]}}

In music, '''flat''' means lower in [[pitch (music)|pitch]]. The flat symbol, {{music|b}}, indicates that the [[note (music)|note]] to which it is applied is played one [[chromatic semitone]] lower. The opposite of flat is [[sharp (music)|sharp]], indicating a raising of pitch.

