[[File:Destruction of Leviathan.png|thumb|upright=1.1|''The Destruction of Leviathan'' by [[Gustave Doré]] (1865)]]
[[File:Babylon's world view.jpg|thumb|upright=1.4|The circular salt sea [[Tiamat]] (green) and sphere of cosmic freshwater ocean [[Abzu]] are pre-existent deities in [[Sumerian literature|Sumerian]] myths, from whose mating Earth was created, so the sketch's side view shows the same as [[Babylonian Map of the World|Babylon's map]]. Referring to the [[Atra-Hasis]] epic, Abzu is the celestial reservoir from which an organisation of younger gods triggers the Flood in order to consume humanity – also a source for the dangerous cosmic sea monster Leviathan.]]

'''Leviathan''' ({{IPAc-en|l|ɪ|ˈ|v|aɪ|.|ə|θ|ən|audio=LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-leviathan.wav}} {{respell|le|VIE|ə|thən}}; {{langx|he|לִוְיָתָן|Livyaṯān}}; {{langx|el|Λεβιάθαν}}) is a giant [[sea serpent]] noted in theology and mythology. It is referenced in  the [[Hebrew Bible]], as a metaphor for a powerful enemy, notably [[Babylon]]. It is referred to in [[Psalms]], the [[Book of Job]], the [[Book of Isaiah]], and the [[Book of Enoch]].<ref>{{cite book |last1=Miller |first1=Robert D. |editor1-last=Grafius |editor1-first=Brandon R. |editor2-last=Morehead |editor2-first=John W. |title=The Oxford Handbook of Biblical Monsters |date=2025 |publisher=Oxford University Press |location=Oxford |isbn=9780197565087 |pages=174–185 |chapter=Leviathan |doi=10.1093/oxfordhb/9780197565056.013.0009}}</ref> Leviathan is often an embodiment of chaos, threatening to eat the damned when their lives are over. In the end, it is annihilated. Christian theologians identified Leviathan with the [[demon]] of the [[seven deadly sins|deadly sin]] ''[[envy]]''. According to [[Ophite Diagrams]], Leviathan encapsulates the space of the material world.

In [[Gnosticism]], it encompasses the world like a sphere and incorporates the souls of those who are too attached to material things, so they cannot reach the [[Pleroma|realm of God's fullness]] beyond, from which all good emanates. In [[Thomas Hobbes|Hobbes]], Leviathan becomes a metaphor for the omnipotence of the state, which maintains itself by educating children in its favour, generation after generation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Goff |first=Graham |date=2022-04-01 |title=Transforming Leviathan: Job, Hobbes, Zvyagintsev and Philosophical Progression |url=https://digitalcommons.unomaha.edu/jrf/vol26/iss1/53 |journal=Journal of Religion & Film |volume=26 |issue=1 |doi=10.32873/uno.dc.jrf.26.01.53 |issn=1092-1311|doi-access=free }}</ref> This idea of eternal power that 'feeds' on its constantly self-produced citizens is based on a concept of conditioning that [[Imprinting (psychology)|imprints]] the human's conscience in a mechanical manner. It deals in a good and evil dualism: A speculative [[natural law]] according to which man should behave towards man [[Homo homini lupus|like a ravenous wolf]], and the pedagogically transmitted laws of the state as Leviathan, whose justification for existence is seen in containing such frightening conditions.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Hobbes |title=Leviathan |chapter=Chapter 30}}</ref>

Leviathan in the [[Book of Job]] is a reflection of the older [[Canaanite religion|Canaanite]] ''[[Lotan]]'', a primeval monster defeated by the god [[Baal Hadad]].<ref>{{cite magazine |first=Charles F. |last=Pfeiffer |year=1960 |title=Lotan and Leviathan |magazine=The Evangelical Quarterly |volume=34 |issue=2 |pages=208–211 |url=https://biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/eq/1960-4_208.pdf |via=Theology on the Web }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |first=Mark R. |last=Sneed |year=2022 |chapter=The Israelite reconfiguration of the Canaanite combat myth: Leviathan |title=Taming the Beast: A reception history of Behemoth and Leviathan |publisher=de Gruyter |location=Berlin, DE }}</ref> Parallels to the role the primeval Sumerian sea goddess [[Tiamat]], who was defeated by [[Marduk]], have long been drawn in [[comparative mythology]], as have been comparisons to [[dragon]] and [[cosmic serpent|world serpent]] narratives, such as [[Indra]] slaying [[Vritra]] or [[Thor]] slaying [[Jörmungandr]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Cirlot |first=Juan Eduardo |author-link=Juan Eduardo Cirlot |year=1971 |title=A Dictionary of Symbols |publisher=Dorset Press |edition=2nd |page=186 }}</ref> Some 19th&nbsp;century scholars pragmatically interpreted it as referring to large aquatic creatures, such as the [[crocodile]].<ref name=Gesenius>{{cite book |first=Wilhelm |last=Gesenius |author-link=Wilhelm Gesenius |translator-first=Samuel Prideaux |translator-last=Tregelles |year=1879 |title=Hebrew and Chaldee lexicon to the Old Testament }}</ref> The word later came to be used as a term for ''great [[whale]]'' and for [[sea monster]]s in general.

{{Short description|In astronomy, angular separation between the Sun and a planet}}
{{other uses|Elongation (disambiguation)}}
{{more citations needed|date=September 2024}}
[[File:Positional astronomy.svg|thumb|350px|This diagram shows various possible elongations (ε), each of which is the [[angular distance]] between a [[planet]] and the [[Sun]] from Earth's perspective.|alt=An angle ε is drawn between two straight lines from Earth to the Sun, and from Earth to the planet. This is demonstrated for different positions along circular orbits, both for planets closer to the Sun (where the angle is always less than 90°), and for outer planets (for which the angle can range from 0° to 180°), while distinguishing east and west sides.]]

In [[astronomy]], a planet's '''elongation''' is the [[angular separation]] between the [[Sun]] and the planet, with [[Earth]] as the [[vertex (geometry)|reference point]].<ref>{{cite EB1911 |wstitle=Elongation |volume=9 |page=298}}</ref>

The '''greatest elongation''' is the maximum angular separation. [[ephemeris|Astronomical tables]] and websites, such as [[Heavens-Above]], forecast when and where the planets reach their next maximum elongations.

Sometimes '''elongation''' may instead refer to the angular distance of the [[Moon]] relative Earth or the [[natural satellite]] of another planet from its central planet, for instance the angular distance of [[Io (moon)|Io]] from [[Jupiter]].

A [[Quadrature (astronomy)|quadrature]] occurs when the position of a body (moon or planet) is such that its elongation is 90° or 270°; i.e. the body-earth-sun angle is 90°.

